<section class="mechanism section-block" aria-labelledby="mechanism-heading">
  <div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">01 / Credential issuance</p><h2 id="mechanism-heading">How SPIRE issues credentials</h2></div><p>SPIFFE supports <a href="/ecosystem/">multiple implementations</a>. This example uses SPIRE, the project’s open-source reference implementation, to show how X.509-SVIDs are issued and renewed.</p></div>
  <figure class="issuance-figure">
    <div class="identity-flow">
      <section><p class="eyebrow">Issuance and rotation</p><h3>SPIRE Server → Attested Agent</h3><p>SPIRE attests nodes and workloads and determines which identities they may receive. The server issues X.509-SVIDs; the agent renews them before expiration.</p></section>
      <section><p class="eyebrow">Local Workload API</p><h3>Agent → Your workload</h3><p>The agent delivers authorized SVIDs, private keys and trust bundles to applications through the SPIFFE Workload API.</p></section>
      <section><p class="eyebrow">Application connection</p><h3>Your workload ↔ Peer service</h3><p>Applications use X.509-SVIDs to authenticate TLS connections and prove possession of their private keys. Receiving services apply their own access policies.</p></section>
    </div>
    <figcaption><span>X.509-SVID example. Credential renewal runs independently of application connections.</span><a href="/docs/spire/about/concepts/">Attestation and issuance in detail →</a></figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>
